.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1049 PM PDT Fri Aug 14 2026
(Tonight through Sunday)

The region will remain on the eastern periphery of upper-level
troughing well off the West Coast Saturday. Sensible weather wise,
this`ll bring another Fogust day with temperatures well below
normal, clouds in the evenings/mornings with a chance for drizzle,
and onshore flow. Gusts of 20-30 mph can be expected through
gaps/passes and down the Salinas Valley. While the marine layer
remains deep, it doesn`t look healthy on satellite with patchwork
stratus across the region. Diurnal winds will keep smoke from the
Timber Fire out to sea with downsloping (easterly) winds in the
morning and onshore (northwesterly) winds in the afternoon.

&&

.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1049 PM PDT Fri Aug 14 2026
(Sunday night through next Friday)

Upper-level ridging will nose in from the east on Sunday and
ultimately anchor itself across the Desert Southwest on Tuesday.
This will result in a gradual warming and drying trend into next
week. Temperatures will moderate to near seasonal normals away from
and above the marine layer influence at the coast. As the marine
layer compresses with subsidence aloft, its footprint will become
smaller and conditions above it will become drier. Poor afternoon
daytime relative humidity of 20-30% is expected for the higher
terrain with locally lower values of 10-20% across the Central
Coast. Recovery will remain moderate through the weekend, but by
Monday little-to-none is expected with that trend continuing through
Friday. This will likely lead to seasonably elevated conditions with
rapid rates of spread on any wind-and-terrain aligned starts in
receptive vegetation. The timeframe of most concern will be with the
diurnal sea breeze in the afternoons/evenings.

